{
  "image_name": "86a7356c6e79edae7424bb4c99a4cb71.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure gives a broad overview of how interfaces convert energy between light, electricity, and chemistry. Across the top, it contrasts optical-to-electrical devices such as photodiodes and phototransistors, showing layered semiconductor structures and how light creates charge carriers. It also compares a traditional p–n junction with a porosity-based junction, where nanoporous silicon in contact with saline appears to shift the band structure and create a different charge-separation behavior at the interface.\n\nThe middle row moves to chemical-to-electrical conversion. A fuel cell is shown driving electrons through an external load while ions move through an electrolyte between anode and cathode. A battery panel illustrates reversible charging and discharging, with ions shuttling through the electrolyte and electrons flowing in the outer circuit. A bio-battery extends this idea to a biological setting, seemingly using ionic gradients and extracellular electrolyte in tissue.\n\nThe bottom row summarizes electrical-to-chemical processes. A faradaic mechanism transfers electrons directly in redox reactions at an electrode, while a capacitive process stores charge in interfacial layers without full redox conversion. The last panel shows catalysis lowering the activation-energy barrier, making chemical reactions proceed more easily while the overall energy difference between reactants and products remains similar.",
